Sorting algorithms are evaluated by how their runtime grows as the input size increases. Big O notation describes this upper-bound growth. For example, Bubble Sort compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are out of order. In the worst case, it performs n^2 comparisons, giving it a time complexity of O(n^2). Merge Sort, on the other hand, divides the list in half repeatedly and merges sorted sublists, achieving O(n log n) in all cases. Understanding these basics helps you choose the right algorithm for a given dataset. Here is a simple Python example of Bubble Sort:

```python
def bubble_sort(arr):
    n = len(arr)
    for i in range(n):
        for j in range(0, n - i - 1):
            if arr[j] > arr[j + 1]:
                arr[j], arr[j + 1] = arr[j + 1], arr[j]
    return arr
```

Notice how the nested loops lead to quadratic growth.

